rent alphabet it's written  from right to left the the syntax of the  grammar is completely different is that  always the case just that the closer the  language is to what you speak the easier  it is  always the case  the biggest issue in learning languages  is words vocabulary so if you're going  from spanish to portuguese you've got 95  of the vocabulary with french you've got  90 or whatever the number is yeah  english to spanish you got 50 of the  vocabulary because of the common latin  origin of so much vocabulary so common  vocabulary is the biggest  because language learning is about  learning words if we have a lot of words  we the brain starts to figure out how  the language works but it's the words  that we need to have the other thing is  writing system obviously if it's written  in the latin alphabet i mean i've  learned you know chinese japanese arabic  persian now hebrew i tried to i mean  where the korean where the language  where the writing system is different  it's a lot more difficult because  everything is habit and we're so used to  reading in our own alphabet and even if  we understand how the symbols work in  hebrew  um  until our brain really gets used to  reading in that language it takes me you  got to read millions of words so yes  familia similarity of of vocabulary and  obviously the same writing system going  to make it a lot easier are there  objectively  more difficult languages to learn  regardless from what language you're  coming from in other words i i think  like one question would be if you speak  english what are the easiest languages  to learn that's one question but but a  more another question would be are there  just objectively more difficult  languages  well i'll give you a good example for an  english speaker as i am i find slavic  languages complicated in their grammar  but if you are a speaker of a sli if  you're a polish speaker and you want to  learn you know ukrainian it's not  difficult  because you're already use