ust  noise and a bunch of squigglies  but if you just stay with it the brain  it's got to trust your brain you're  going to trust your brain your brain  will get used to it  i don't know how much you know about how  language is taught  in the united states in general in  middle school and high school but one of  the things i've observed is that many of  the people who take a few years of a  second language in the united states  don't really come out speaking very much  at all and some of the things i observed  in my high school for example with the  spanish teachers was  many of them were not only non-native  speakers they weren't even really fluent  they spoke with with very poor accents  and grammatically it was like 90 percent  of the way there so okay that seems to  me like a recipe for disaster if the  person teaching doesn't speak that well  it's going to be hard to learn but can  you comment a little bit about how  language is taught to middle and high  schoolers and and whether it's being  done the right way or not  well and even a worse example is the  english school system in canada where  kids have  english for excuse me have french for  let's say 10 years and can't speak ah  and  the thing is that first of all  motivation and the attitude of the  learner is so important so the most  important thing for the teacher is to be  able to motivate the learner we don't  need the teacher to become the model of  the language particularly today there's  so much stuff out there  there's some there's  netflix and youtube and podcasts and  radio and tv we don't need the teacher  to be the model of the language but the  teacher has to motivate the learner you  don't even really need the teacher to  teach the grammar you can look it up if  i want to know the conjugation of a verb  in spanish i can look it up if i'm  curious if i'm motivated so the key  thing is motivation and that's where the  way languages are taught typically is  quite demotivating because you're taught  some rules of grammar you're tested on  it you get it wrong  getting things wrong is not very  motivating if you can spend the time  listening to things of interest to you  reading  getting the language in you  more more students will learn to speak  well some some students don't want to  learn but it's the whole idea can you  increase the number of people who are  motivated enough to learn that they will  actually go out and learn  last thing i want to ask you about very  anecdotally  i'm curious whether  there is a linguistic or societal  explanation for why some folks seem to  be almost like predisposed to better  learn  some languages like english and i'll  give you an example i've this is very  anecdotal and i'm going to you just for  you to tell me it's only anecdotal or  there's something to it i've met a  number of folks from the philippines  when i was in france when i was in the  uk  who  spoke english as though they were native  american individuals and they told me  i've never even been to the united  states i learned english merely by  watching american films and television  shows and kind of teaching myself and to  me they sounded like native american  english speakers is there something for  example about the filipino language or  the people that might predispose them to  be better at learning english is there  something to that  i don't think so i mean we have a lot of  filipino immigrants here in vancouver  and a lot of them speak with a  noticeable accent which they never get  rid of  it's rather the the access to american  tv programs if you go to sweden  everyone speaks english it's not because  they teach english better in their  schools it's because all television  programs are in the original version so  they watch lots of american and british  television it's kind of natural to them  that's part of almost part of the  culture they grow up with so  again it comes back to input  uh you know if you're uh if you're an  english-speaking canadi